On Mon, Jun 16, 2014 at 8:23 AM, Creative Care Services <
comcaresvcs@gmail.com> wrote:

> Wait a minute.... At the end of the included post Jorge makes a statement
> I find very wrong. He says he doesn't see a problem using kavbu for the
> phrase "caught a cold." I do not believe this is the concept behind kavbu,
> and I find it horribly malglico. You can certainly catch things
> unintentionally, like a bug in your mouth when it flys in. The problem I
> have is that a cold is not something you catch in concept, but rather
> something that invades or attacks you. "Catch a cold" is an idiom.
>

We were discussing the property-as-possession metaphor. If you can
exchange/inherit/lose/reword with/regard as precious/regard as
costly/give/offer/price/earn/win/pay for/profit with/value/sell/buy a
property, which the gismu list explicitly recommends, then why would you
not be able to catch one? It's the same metaphor. But you wouldn't really
catch a cold, you would catch having a cold. ("kavbu lo ka ce'u bilma", not
"kavbu lo te bilma". The latter would be a different type of metaphor.

mu'o mi'e xorxes

